WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:06.520
The next data manipulation keyword we are
going to look at is the DELETE keyword.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.490
The DELETE keyword deletes records from a
table.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.440
It does not delete an object such as a table.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.100
If you want to delete an entire object, you
will want to use the DROP keyword, which is

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.040
covered elsewhere in this course.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.750
I have a file open that has both a SELECT
statement and a DELETE statement.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:29.500
Before running a DELETE statement, it is always
a good idea to run a SELECT statement on the

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.610
table you are about to change.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:36.489
Doing this will ensure it is the table you
want to delete records from because, as stated

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:40.469
elsewhere in this course, these changes cannot
be undone.

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:46.920
I will run the SELECT statement, and indeed
this is the table that has the records I want

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.820
to delete.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.399
The DELETE statement itself is very simple.

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:56.420
All this contains is DELETE FROM and then
the table to be emptied.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:02.449
If we wanted to use a WHERE clause to limit
what is being deleted, we can.

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:09.930
That said, I will select and execute this
DELETE statement, and this table has been

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:10.580
emptied.

